\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 In Jewish law, a transfer of ownership is not considered complete unless both parties clearly recognize and accept the transaction. This idea is expressed through Kinyan, a formal act that confirms the agreement and removes uncertainty. Traditional methods like Kinyan Sudar, where an item is exchanged symbolically, or Meshicha, where property is physically pulled or moved, were designed to make the transfer visible and socially recognized.\
Bitcoin\'92s blockchain system reflects some of these same concerns in a digital setting. Once a transaction is verified and added to the blockchain, it becomes extremely difficult to reverse or alter. That permanent public record can be compared to the sense of finality that halachic systems try to establish through Kinyan.\
The blockchain also creates transparency because transactions can be publicly viewed and traced through the UTXO model. In some ways, this connects to the halachic concept of Ona\'92ah, which focuses on preventing unfair business practices and deception in commerce. By making transaction history openly accessible, Bitcoin reduces some forms of hidden manipulation and creates a clearer framework for exchange.\cf4 \strokec4 \
